---
title: Bitter leaf salad
description: Lesson 6.1. Tasting in pairs, and what salt does to bitterness.
servings: 2
time: 15 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, tasting-and-fixing]
lesson: "6.1"
technique: seasoning with salt
status: draft
---

> Technique: tasting in pairs. To learn what an adjustment does, taste
> the same food before it and after it, one change at a time. This
> lesson does it with salt, on a leaf bitter enough to make the
> difference plain.

Separate @chicory{2%heads}(or 1 small radicchio) into leaves. Tear off a piece of leaf and eat it plain. Notice how bitter it is.

Put a few grains of @flaky salt{1/4%tsp} on a second piece and eat that. Compare.

> Science: salt does more than add saltiness. In taste experiments,
> sodium salts cut the bitterness of urea by over 70%, and the effect
> came from the sodium ion acting at the taste cells, not from the
> brain being distracted. It is selective: the bitterness of magnesium
> sulphate was not reduced at all. So whether salt tames a given
> bitter food is something to test on your own tongue, which is what
> you just did. Look up: bitterness suppression, sodium, taste
> interactions.
-- source: Keast, Breslin and Beauchamp, "Suppression of Bitterness Using Sodium Salts", Chimia 55(5):441, 2001, https://doi.org/10.2533/chimia.2001.441 (abstract)

Cut the peel and pith off @orange{1}. Cut out the segments over a #large bowl{} to catch the juice, then squeeze the rest of the orange into the bowl.

Whisk @olive oil{2%tbsp} and the rest of the salt into the juice. Add the leaves and the orange segments and toss.

> Why orange: it brings sweetness and acid. In the same experiments,
> when salt suppressed the bitterness in a bitter and sweet mixture,
> the sweetness came through more strongly. An acid is the other
> standard answer to bitterness.
-- source: Keast, Breslin and Beauchamp, "Suppression of Bitterness Using Sodium Salts", Chimia 55(5):441, 2001, https://doi.org/10.2533/chimia.2001.441 (abstract) ; Rebecca Hays, "Is Your Dish Too Salty? Sweet? Sour? Spicy? Here's How to Fix It.", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-07-04, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/5816-is-your-dish-too-salty-sweet-sour-spicy-here's-how-to-fix-it (too bitter: "Add salt or an acid")

Taste a dressed leaf. If the bitterness still leads, add a pinch more salt, toss and taste again. Stop when the bitterness sits behind the orange. Finish with @black pepper{}.

> Why a pinch at a time: you can always add more, and you cannot take
> it out.
-- source: "Seasoning Fixes", America's Test Kitchen, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/lessons/articles/2287-seasoning-fixes

> Next time, without the recipe: before you add anything to a dish,
> taste it. Add one thing, a little, and taste again. With bitter
> food, try salt first.
